latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Shapes:\n   - Use a flood fill algorithm to find all connected non-zero areas in the grid.\n   - For each shape, store its color, coordinates, and bounding box (min/max row and column).\n\n2. Define Wave Function:\n   - Create a function that takes as input:\n     a. The shape's bounding box dimensions\n     b. The current point's position within the shape\n     c. The point's distance from the shape's center\n   - The function should return a horizontal shift value (positive for right, negative for left).\n   - The wave function should produce larger shifts near the center and smaller shifts near the top and bottom.\n\n3. Process Each Shape:\n   - For each identified shape:\n     a. Calculate the shape's center point.\n     b. For each point in the shape:\n       - Calculate its relative position within the shape.\n       - Apply the wave function to determine the shift.\n       - Store the proposed new position for this point.\n\n4. Apply Transformations:\n   - Create a new grid of the same size as the input.\n   - For each shape, in order of identification:\n     a. For each point in the shape:\n       - Check if the new position is valid (within grid bounds and not overlapping another shape).\n       - If valid, place the point in its new position on the new grid.\n       - If not valid, try to place it as close as possible to the intended position while maintaining connectivity.\n\n5. Maintain Connectivity:\n   - After placing all points for a shape:\n     a. Check for any disconnected segments.\n     b. If found, fill in gaps using the shape's color to maintain vertical and horizontal connectivity.\n\n6. Handle Edge Cases:\n   - For points on the top and bottom rows of each shape:\n     a. Allow slight modifications (e.g., small inward shifts) based on the wave function, but with reduced intensity.\n   - For points on the left and right edges:\n     a. Allow outward bulges if space permits, based on the wave function.\n\n7. Preserve Background:\n   - Ensure all background (0) areas in the original grid remain unchanged in the new grid.\n\n8. Final Pass:\n   - Scan the entire new grid:\n     a. Check for any unintended isolated points or disconnected segments.\n     b. If found, adjust them to connect with their nearest neighbors of the same color.\n\n9. Output:\n   - Return the new grid as the transformed result.\n\nThis plan allows for the organic, wave-like transformations seen in all examples, while maintaining the overall structure and connectivity of the shapes. It's flexible enough to handle both simple and complex cases, and it accounts for the variations in wave intensity and direction observed across different parts of the shapes."
